"""Producer/consumer over a Channel[i32]. The producer sends 0..100 then
closes; the consumer drains with blocking `recv()` until the channel is
closed and empty (`ChannelClosedError`). Demonstrates §16.1 (Thread),
§16.3 (Channel), §7.5 (try/except), and lambda capture per the §21.4
style — `Thread(fn() -> None: producer(ch))`.

The consumer used to poll `try_recv()` and break on `none`, but `none`
means "empty" as well as "closed" (M5 limitation), so a consumer that
polled between sends exited early — and once it was gone the producer
blocked forever on the full bounded channel and `t1.join()` deadlocked
(observed as a rare hang when the test suite runs in parallel). The
recv + except form is the race-free drain protocol."""

# NOTE: §16 declares `Thread` and `Channel` as runtime classes but does
# not pin down their import path. Following the example in §21.4, we
# import from `threading`.
from threading import Thread, Channel

fn producer(ch: Channel[i32]) -> None:
    i: i32 = 0
    while i < 100:
        ch.send(i)
        i = i + 1
    ch.close()

# The consumer deliberately does NOT poll with `try_recv`: it returns the
# same `none` for "empty" and "closed" (BUGS_KNOWN.md BUG-046), so a
# polling consumer can exit early and leave the producer blocked forever
# on a full channel — `t1.join()` in main would then deadlock. Blocking
# `recv()` raises ChannelClosedError exactly when the stream is finished.
fn consumer(ch: Channel[i32]) -> None:
    running: bool = true
    while running:
        try:
            v: i32 = ch.recv()
            println("got " + str(v))
        except ChannelClosedError:
            running = false

fn main() -> i32:
    ch: Channel[i32] = Channel[i32](16)
    t1: Thread = Thread(fn() -> None: producer(ch))
    t2: Thread = Thread(fn() -> None: consumer(ch))
    t1.start()
    t2.start()
    t1.join()
    t2.join()
    return 0
